<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Jammu and Kashmir (princely state)</h1><p class="st-meta">1846 CE – 1952 CE · capital Srinagar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Dogra kingdom created by the Treaty of Amritsar in 1846, whose disputed accession in 1947 began the Kashmir conflict.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulab-singh">Gulab Singh</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dogras">Dogra</a> noble who had served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> and became Raja of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a>, extended his rule to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ladakh">Ladakh</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-sikh-war">First Anglo-Sikh War</a>, the British sold him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> valley by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-amritsar-1846">Treaty of Amritsar</a> in 1846, creating a large state under the Dogra dynasty.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dogras">Dogra</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharajas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> rulers of a Muslim-majority population, governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ladakh">Ladakh</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit">Gilgit</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranbir-singh-of-jammu-and-kashmir">Ranbir Singh</a> codified laws; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pratap-singh-of-jammu-and-kashmir">Pratap Singh</a> accepted British supervision. Heavy taxes and discrimination against Muslims provoked the 1931 agitation that brought Sheikh Abdullah to prominence. Kashmiri <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawl">shawls</a>, saffron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> were key products.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hari-singh">Hari Singh</a> delayed choosing between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Pakistan</a> in 1947. After a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pashtun</a> tribal invasion he acceded to India in October 1947, triggering war and a ceasefire in 1949 that divided the state. The monarchy was abolished in 1952.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jammu-and-kashmir-princely-state.jpg" alt="Jammu and Kashmir (princely state)" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Zuse · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Maharaja_of_Jammu_and_Kashmir_(1846-1936).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1846 CE – 1952 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Srinagar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gulab Singh, Ranbir Singh, Hari Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam; Hinduism; Buddhism (Ladakh)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kashmiri, Dogri, Ladakhi, Urdu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a>, <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, <a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jammu and Kashmir, Kashmir and Jammu, Princely State of Kashmir</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1899&amp;focus=jammu-and-kashmir-princely-state" aria-label="Jammu and Kashmir (princely state) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6585 942 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jammu and Kashmir (princely state) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6905 1198L6918 1285M6905 1198L7364 1601M6905 1198L7364 1602M6905 1198L7018 1386M6905 1198L6811 1139M6905 1198L6879 1123M6905 1198L6866 1154M6905 1198L6890 1209M6905 1198L6928 1234"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6918 1285h0M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7018 1386h0M6811 1139h0M6879 1123h0M6866 1154h0M6890 1209h0M6928 1234h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6905 1198h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6905 1198h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Srinagar. <a href="/atlas?year=1899&amp;focus=jammu-and-kashmir-princely-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
